{"journal_version":1,"sequence":5,"replace_from":2,"message_count":3,"messages":[{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need answer verdict. Need inspect diff only no tools. Need find concrete regression/unmet criterion. Let's analyze carefully.\n\nTask acceptance:\n- input accessible label Search tasks and search role. type=search aria-label yes.\n- filtering immediate case-insensitive titles. Code uses query.trim().toLowerCase(); title.toLowerCase().includes(normalizedQuery). If query non-empty whitespace only, normalizedQuery '' => no filter. Non-empty query shows Clear search button: showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible. For whitespace query, input has non-empty query, showClear true, but filtering not applied. Acceptance \"Filtering is immediate and case-insensitive against task titles.\" For whitespace? Maybe okay? But \"non-empty query shows Clear search\" yes. Potential subtle: trim means query with leading/trailing spaces filters trimmed query, okay.\n\n- clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button. Code: clearSearch setQuery(''); setKeepClearVisible(true). Button remains mounted due keepClearVisible. But React state update: button remains same element? It is conditional `{showClear && <button ...>}`. showClear was true before, after true because keepClearVisible true, so same DOM button likely retained, focus remains. Good. However issue: onBlur sets keepClearVisible false. When clicking Clear search, does button blur? Clicking button itself does not blur before onClick? Focus goes to button on mousedown; onClick then set state. It remains. Good. After clearing, focus stays on button. But showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible means after clearing it remains until blur. That's intentional.\n\n- If no task matches, show exactly No tasks match your search. Code shows p when visibleTasks.length===0. But this also occurs when tasks array is empty and query empty! Existing behavior previously rendered empty `<ul className=\"task-list\"></ul>` for empty task list. New code shows \"No tasks match your search.\" when `tasks.length === 0` even with no query. That is a regression in empty state: empty task list now shows search no-match message, which is semantically wrong and changes existing behavior. Acceptance says \"If no task matches, show exactly...\" Could include empty tasks no query? But no task matches search? The message should likely only when query non-empty. The developer report says \"When nothing matches, list replaced...\" But acceptance specifically if no task matches. Could be interpreted as no query also? However \"Search\" message when no tasks exist is a UI state regression. Need decide FAIL? The prompt says focus old behavior and UI states not covered; loading/error/empty transitions. This is concrete: existing empty list renders empty list; now displays \"No tasks match your search\" for empty tasks even when no search query. Is there existing empty state elsewhere? We only have diff. The original `tasks.map` produced empty ul. This new p changes empty state. Is that a regression? It might be acceptable? Acceptance says \"If no task matches, show exactly No tasks match your search.\" For empty tasks, no task matches any search, but no query. Usually empty list should not show search-empty. But public tests might not cover. It's a concrete behavior change. Need see if meets \"preserve all existing behavior\". It doesn't if tasks empty. Potential FAIL severity medium. Need name exact diff evidence. Repro: render TaskList tasks={[]} selectedId null; no query; observe \"No tasks match your search.\" instead of empty ul. Bounded fix: only show no-match message when `normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0`; otherwise render empty ul or existing empty mark. But if query whitespace, showClear true but no filter; maybe no-match only normalizedQuery. That fixes empty state. But is it within acceptance? If no tasks and query empty, should not show message. Good.\n\nAnother issue: selected task hidden. The selected-task detail card in App presumably uses selectedId and full tasks, unaffected. Good.\n\nKeyboard/focus: Clear button persists after clearing; type=search native clear button? Not relevant. Accessibility label yes. Button has no aria-label but text Clear search okay.\n\nCancellation/persistence/responsive: local state fine.\n\nNeed choose verdict. Must first line exactly VERDICT: FAIL or PASS. We have concrete regression. Is it enough? The prompt says choose FAIL only for concrete repairable regression or unmet acceptance criterion supported by diff. This is supported. Need answer with severity, affected behavior, exact diff evidence, reproduction, bounded fix. No extra? Should be concise. They said first line must be exactly. Final answer include. Need not overdo. Ensure no invented. Let's formulate.\n\nPotential another regression: For query that is non-empty whitespace, show Clear search, but filtering not applied (visibleTasks = tasks because normalizedQuery empty). Acceptance \"Filtering is immediate\" maybe query whitespace should filter by space? But trimming is common. Not mention.\n\nAnother issue: \"A non-empty query shows a Clear search button; clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button.\" After clearing, button stays mounted, but `onBlur` releases `keepClearVisible`. If user clicks Clear, focus remains on button; if user then uses Tab, button blur sets false, unmounts, focus moves? Tab from button when unmounted? If button loses focus due tab, onBlur unmounts it, focus goes to next element? That's okay. But if user presses Enter on Clear while focused, onClick sets keepClear true and query ''. Focus stays. Good.\n\nPotential issue: While query non-empty, if selected task hidden, selected detail remains. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Clear search\" button uses className icon-button, no CSS but okay.\n\nPotential issue: The empty no-match message appears when tasks array empty even query non-empty too; that's acceptance. But when no query, wrong.\n\nCould the App always has tasks nonempty? Not guaranteed. Existing TaskList supported empty tasks. Acceptance says preserve existing behavior. So fail.\n\nNeed maybe mention severity: Medium. Affected behavior: empty task list initial/after deleting all tasks shows no-match search message even though no search active, misrepresenting empty state. Exact evidence: outer `{visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : (<ul ...` with `visibleTasks = normalizedQuery ? ... : tasks`, so when `tasks.length === 0` and query empty, `visibleTasks.length === 0`. Repro: render TaskList tasks={[]} query empty. Bounded fix: gate message on `normalizedQuery.length > 0`, otherwise render original `<ul className=\"task-list\">` (empty) or appropriate existing empty state. Need be careful: if query empty and tasks empty, original empty ul. Or show no-match only when query non-empty. But whitespace query? If query is \"   \", normalized empty but query nonempty and showClear true. Filtering no change. Should it show no-match? No because no filtering applied. So gate on normalizedQuery !== ''.\n\nWould this violate if tasks nonempty and query whitespace? show clear yes. Fine.\n\nMaybe there is a bug with `showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible`: after clearing, query '', keepClearVisible true. If tasks change? Not relevant. If input changed then keep false. On blur false. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Clear search button; clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button.\" If user clears by clicking the native type=search clear (X) in browser, onChange fires setQuery('') and setKeepClearVisible(false), showClear false, button unmounts. But acceptance \"clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button\" refers Clear search button not native clear. But adding type=search may expose native clear control; if user uses it, button disappears and focus not on button. Is that an acceptance violation? It says A non-empty query shows a Clear search button; clearing... leaves focus on that button. Could imply using the Clear search button. Not necessarily native. Not concrete.\n\nPotential issue: Query is local; if selected task row hidden and user edits? Not possible hidden. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: Empty message \"exactly No tasks match your search.\" It is in p exactly. Good.\n\nPotential issue: filter case-insensitive against titles; trim lower. Good.\n\nCould decide PASS? Need weigh. The prompt's examples include empty transitions; this is a clear oversight. The developer report says \"When nothing matches, the list is replaced...\" They didn't mention empty task list. Acceptance \"If no task matches\" ambiguous but no query empty list not a search. Also \"Preserve all existing behavior.\" This is a regression. So FAIL.\n\nNeed final with exact first line. Ensure no extra preamble. Maybe use bullets. Desired oververbosity 5. We have limited tokens but enough. Need mention no public tests. We should be direct.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nVERDICT: FAIL\n\n- Severity: Medium\n- Affected behavior: Empty task list without an active search now renders the search-specific no-match message; previously it rendered the empty `ul.task-list`. This also affects initial empty state / after all tasks removed.\n- Diff evidence: `const visibleTasks = normalizedQuery ? ... : tasks;` then unconditional `{visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : ...}`. When `tasks=[]` and `query=''`, `normalizedQuery=''`, `visibleTasks.length===0`, so message shows despite no query.\n- Reproduction: Render `TaskList` with `tasks={[]}` and no user input (or empty search). Observe \"No tasks match your search.\" instead of empty task list.\n- Bounded fix: Only render the no-match paragraph when a normalized query is active, e.g. `normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0`; otherwise render the original empty `<ul className=\"task-list\">` (or existing empty state).\n\nDo we need mention \"exactly\"? yes.\n\nMaybe there is another concern: If `tasks=[]` and query non-empty, should show no-match (yes). If query whitespace, showClear true but normalized empty; with fix not show no-match; empty list shows empty ul. Good.\n\nNeed ensure first line exact `VERDICT: FAIL` no backticks. Final.","content":"VERDICT: FAIL\n\n- **Severity:** Medium\n- **Affected behavior:** An empty task list with no active search now shows the search-specific empty message, changing existing behavior. This affects initial empty states and the state after all tasks are removed.\n- **Diff evidence:** `visibleTasks` falls back to `tasks` when `normalizedQuery` is empty, but the render condition is unconditional: `visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : ...`. With `tasks=[]` and `query=''`, `visibleTasks.length === 0`, so the no-match message renders even though no search is active.\n- **Reproduction:** Render `TaskList` with `tasks={[]}` and no user input. Observe `No tasks match your search.` instead of the previous empty task-list markup.\n- **Bounded fix:** Only show the search no-match message when a normalized query is active:\n\n```tsx\n{normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (\n  <p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>\n) : (\n  <ul className=\"task-list\">\n    {visibleTasks.map(...)}\n  </ul>\n)}\n```\n\nThis preserves the original empty-list behavior while still showing the required message for actual searches.","response_items":[{"id":"bc34256e-5992-4264-90e4-c1f5e22b4dff","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","content":[{"text":"We need answer verdict. Need inspect diff only no tools. Need find concrete regression/unmet criterion. Let's analyze carefully.\n\nTask acceptance:\n- input accessible label Search tasks and search role. type=search aria-label yes.\n- filtering immediate case-insensitive titles. Code uses query.trim().toLowerCase(); title.toLowerCase().includes(normalizedQuery). If query non-empty whitespace only, normalizedQuery '' => no filter. Non-empty query shows Clear search button: showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible. For whitespace query, input has non-empty query, showClear true, but filtering not applied. Acceptance \"Filtering is immediate and case-insensitive against task titles.\" For whitespace? Maybe okay? But \"non-empty query shows Clear search\" yes. Potential subtle: trim means query with leading/trailing spaces filters trimmed query, okay.\n\n- clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button. Code: clearSearch setQuery(''); setKeepClearVisible(true). Button remains mounted due keepClearVisible. But React state update: button remains same element? It is conditional `{showClear && <button ...>}`. showClear was true before, after true because keepClearVisible true, so same DOM button likely retained, focus remains. Good. However issue: onBlur sets keepClearVisible false. When clicking Clear search, does button blur? Clicking button itself does not blur before onClick? Focus goes to button on mousedown; onClick then set state. It remains. Good. After clearing, focus stays on button. But showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible means after clearing it remains until blur. That's intentional.\n\n- If no task matches, show exactly No tasks match your search. Code shows p when visibleTasks.length===0. But this also occurs when tasks array is empty and query empty! Existing behavior previously rendered empty `<ul className=\"task-list\"></ul>` for empty task list. New code shows \"No tasks match your search.\" when `tasks.length === 0` even with no query. That is a regression in empty state: empty task list now shows search no-match message, which is semantically wrong and changes existing behavior. Acceptance says \"If no task matches, show exactly...\" Could include empty tasks no query? But no task matches search? The message should likely only when query non-empty. The developer report says \"When nothing matches, list replaced...\" But acceptance specifically if no task matches. Could be interpreted as no query also? However \"Search\" message when no tasks exist is a UI state regression. Need decide FAIL? The prompt says focus old behavior and UI states not covered; loading/error/empty transitions. This is concrete: existing empty list renders empty list; now displays \"No tasks match your search\" for empty tasks even when no search query. Is there existing empty state elsewhere? We only have diff. The original `tasks.map` produced empty ul. This new p changes empty state. Is that a regression? It might be acceptable? Acceptance says \"If no task matches, show exactly No tasks match your search.\" For empty tasks, no task matches any search, but no query. Usually empty list should not show search-empty. But public tests might not cover. It's a concrete behavior change. Need see if meets \"preserve all existing behavior\". It doesn't if tasks empty. Potential FAIL severity medium. Need name exact diff evidence. Repro: render TaskList tasks={[]} selectedId null; no query; observe \"No tasks match your search.\" instead of empty ul. Bounded fix: only show no-match message when `normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0`; otherwise render empty ul or existing empty mark. But if query whitespace, showClear true but no filter; maybe no-match only normalizedQuery. That fixes empty state. But is it within acceptance? If no tasks and query empty, should not show message. Good.\n\nAnother issue: selected task hidden. The selected-task detail card in App presumably uses selectedId and full tasks, unaffected. Good.\n\nKeyboard/focus: Clear button persists after clearing; type=search native clear button? Not relevant. Accessibility label yes. Button has no aria-label but text Clear search okay.\n\nCancellation/persistence/responsive: local state fine.\n\nNeed choose verdict. Must first line exactly VERDICT: FAIL or PASS. We have concrete regression. Is it enough? The prompt says choose FAIL only for concrete repairable regression or unmet acceptance criterion supported by diff. This is supported. Need answer with severity, affected behavior, exact diff evidence, reproduction, bounded fix. No extra? Should be concise. They said first line must be exactly. Final answer include. Need not overdo. Ensure no invented. Let's formulate.\n\nPotential another regression: For query that is non-empty whitespace, show Clear search, but filtering not applied (visibleTasks = tasks because normalizedQuery empty). Acceptance \"Filtering is immediate\" maybe query whitespace should filter by space? But trimming is common. Not mention.\n\nAnother issue: \"A non-empty query shows a Clear search button; clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button.\" After clearing, button stays mounted, but `onBlur` releases `keepClearVisible`. If user clicks Clear, focus remains on button; if user then uses Tab, button blur sets false, unmounts, focus moves? Tab from button when unmounted? If button loses focus due tab, onBlur unmounts it, focus goes to next element? That's okay. But if user presses Enter on Clear while focused, onClick sets keepClear true and query ''. Focus stays. Good.\n\nPotential issue: While query non-empty, if selected task hidden, selected detail remains. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Clear search\" button uses className icon-button, no CSS but okay.\n\nPotential issue: The empty no-match message appears when tasks array empty even query non-empty too; that's acceptance. But when no query, wrong.\n\nCould the App always has tasks nonempty? Not guaranteed. Existing TaskList supported empty tasks. Acceptance says preserve existing behavior. So fail.\n\nNeed maybe mention severity: Medium. Affected behavior: empty task list initial/after deleting all tasks shows no-match search message even though no search active, misrepresenting empty state. Exact evidence: outer `{visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : (<ul ...` with `visibleTasks = normalizedQuery ? ... : tasks`, so when `tasks.length === 0` and query empty, `visibleTasks.length === 0`. Repro: render TaskList tasks={[]} query empty. Bounded fix: gate message on `normalizedQuery.length > 0`, otherwise render original `<ul className=\"task-list\">` (empty) or appropriate existing empty state. Need be careful: if query empty and tasks empty, original empty ul. Or show no-match only when query non-empty. But whitespace query? If query is \"   \", normalized empty but query nonempty and showClear true. Filtering no change. Should it show no-match? No because no filtering applied. So gate on normalizedQuery !== ''.\n\nWould this violate if tasks nonempty and query whitespace? show clear yes. Fine.\n\nMaybe there is a bug with `showClear = query !== '' || keepClearVisible`: after clearing, query '', keepClearVisible true. If tasks change? Not relevant. If input changed then keep false. On blur false. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"Clear search button; clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button.\" If user clears by clicking the native type=search clear (X) in browser, onChange fires setQuery('') and setKeepClearVisible(false), showClear false, button unmounts. But acceptance \"clearing restores every task and leaves focus on that button\" refers Clear search button not native clear. But adding type=search may expose native clear control; if user uses it, button disappears and focus not on button. Is that an acceptance violation? It says A non-empty query shows a Clear search button; clearing... leaves focus on that button. Could imply using the Clear search button. Not necessarily native. Not concrete.\n\nPotential issue: Query is local; if selected task row hidden and user edits? Not possible hidden. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: Empty message \"exactly No tasks match your search.\" It is in p exactly. Good.\n\nPotential issue: filter case-insensitive against titles; trim lower. Good.\n\nCould decide PASS? Need weigh. The prompt's examples include empty transitions; this is a clear oversight. The developer report says \"When nothing matches, the list is replaced...\" They didn't mention empty task list. Acceptance \"If no task matches\" ambiguous but no query empty list not a search. Also \"Preserve all existing behavior.\" This is a regression. So FAIL.\n\nNeed final with exact first line. Ensure no extra preamble. Maybe use bullets. Desired oververbosity 5. We have limited tokens but enough. Need mention no public tests. We should be direct.\n\nLet's craft:\n\nVERDICT: FAIL\n\n- Severity: Medium\n- Affected behavior: Empty task list without an active search now renders the search-specific no-match message; previously it rendered the empty `ul.task-list`. This also affects initial empty state / after all tasks removed.\n- Diff evidence: `const visibleTasks = normalizedQuery ? ... : tasks;` then unconditional `{visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : ...}`. When `tasks=[]` and `query=''`, `normalizedQuery=''`, `visibleTasks.length===0`, so message shows despite no query.\n- Reproduction: Render `TaskList` with `tasks={[]}` and no user input (or empty search). Observe \"No tasks match your search.\" instead of empty task list.\n- Bounded fix: Only render the no-match paragraph when a normalized query is active, e.g. `normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0`; otherwise render the original empty `<ul className=\"task-list\">` (or existing empty state).\n\nDo we need mention \"exactly\"? yes.\n\nMaybe there is another concern: If `tasks=[]` and query non-empty, should show no-match (yes). If query whitespace, showClear true but normalized empty; with fix not show no-match; empty list shows empty ul. Good.\n\nNeed ensure first line exact `VERDICT: FAIL` no backticks. Final.","type":"reasoning_text"}],"encrypted_content":"96b84b36-99c5-43f7-9db1-87e05ca3c052-0","status":"completed"},{"id":"86e8bcc5-38e4-431e-b332-5e4070b1d0e7","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"VERDICT: FAIL\n\n- **Severity:** Medium\n- **Affected behavior:** An empty task list with no active search now shows the search-specific empty message, changing existing behavior. This affects initial empty states and the state after all tasks are removed.\n- **Diff evidence:** `visibleTasks` falls back to `tasks` when `normalizedQuery` is empty, but the render condition is unconditional: `visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (<p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>) : ...`. With `tasks=[]` and `query=''`, `visibleTasks.length === 0`, so the no-match message renders even though no search is active.\n- **Reproduction:** Render `TaskList` with `tasks={[]}` and no user input. Observe `No tasks match your search.` instead of the previous empty task-list markup.\n- **Bounded fix:** Only show the search no-match message when a normalized query is active:\n\n```tsx\n{normalizedQuery !== '' && visibleTasks.length === 0 ? (\n  <p className=\"task-empty\">No tasks match your search.</p>\n) : (\n  <ul className=\"task-list\">\n    {visibleTasks.map(...)}\n  </ul>\n)}\n```\n\nThis preserves the original empty-list behavior while still showing the required message for actual searches.","type":"output_text","logprobs":[]}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":"final_answer"}],"timestamp":"2026-11-22T15:53:34.889031+00:00"}],"meta":{"snapshot_version":1,"aid":1,"role":"workflow_agent","model":"deepseek-flash","session_state":{"used_tokens":4026,"context_tokens":1569,"step_count":1,"markup_recovered":0,"recent_call_hashes":[],"reads_since_last_edit":0,"has_landed_write":false,"low_yield_since_progress":0,"distinct_evidence_count":0,"scout_ledger":[],"steps_since_progress":0,"wind_down_done":false,"wind_down_attempts":0,"wind_down_token_mark":0,"budget_reserve_consumed":false,"loop_blocked_since_progress":0,"loop_state":{"version":1,"edit_seq":0,"blocked_rounds":0,"last_counted_step":-1,"operations":{}},"write_effect_unknown":false,"last_progress_unknown":false,"phase":"done","terminal_reason":"completed","pending_events":[],"pending_external_user_turn":null,"active_turn_start_message_index":null,"completed_turn_start_message_index":2,"pending_step_latency":null,"submitted_summary":null,"anthropic_history":null},"run_id":"workflow-1390c0087ea24b6ab0f005a0eb618918"},"seen_result_hashes_reset":false,"seen_result_hashes_added":[]}
